      "math_academy_id": "q-112145",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 18 \\\\  &  & -2 & 12 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & -6 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+2}={x}^{2}-6x+9\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)({x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(x-3)(x-3)=(x+2)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-2$ and a double root at $x=3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+2)(0-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,18),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
